00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:49.340
So what is Career Conversations?

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:51.720
It's a podcast series
where SOM students sit down

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.570
with alumni for a series
of candid conversations

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:58.440
about career paths, industries,
opportunities for MBAs,

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:01.700
and discussions on various career topics.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:05.030
They really range from work-life balance,

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:08.820
to very specific opportunities
for MBAs within a field,

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:12.670
challenges, and we also usually touch upon

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:15.120
their experience at SOM, and even get

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.800
some faculty shout-outs, and
course shout-outs, as well.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:22.350
And of course, because our
mission is educating leaders

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:24.770
for business and society, it's also

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:27.310
a great platform to reflect that.

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:29.600
And so, often a lot of our questions,

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.780
whether they're direct
or indirect, touch upon

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:35.910
how they are leaders
within that confluence

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:37.223
of business and society.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:43.810
So we're just about to
conclude our first season

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:45.810
of Career Conversations.

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:48.540
We've had a total of seven
guests, there's six here,

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:51.360
because we're just
launching one next week.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:53.770
But you'll see where
we've sort of attempted

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:58.770
to connect current students
with alumni across sectors,

00:04:59.230 --> 00:05:01.253
as well as demographic backgrounds.

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:06.700
So kind of stepping
back, I thought I would

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:09.730
share with you a little bit about

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:13.270
how we approached Career Conversations.

00:05:13.270 --> 00:05:14.590
It didn't happen overnight, obviously,

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:15.890
in fact, it took quite awhile.

00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:18.140
But once we decided we
wanted to do a podcast,

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:19.510
it happened pretty quickly.

00:05:19.510 --> 00:05:21.430
And I think there's a spectrum of ways

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:24.190
to execute upon a launch of a podcast.

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:25.360
I think we landed right in the middle.

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:27.920
We did a lot of strategy,
we, you know, several of us

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:29.430
in the Communications
Department sat in a room,

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:31.820
we brainstormed, and we narrowed it down.

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:34.610
We found a co-producer,
and we worked through it.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:38.760
But I would say, you know,
the more time you can spend

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:43.050
at the strategy part
of your podcast launch,

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:43.883
the better you'll be.

00:05:43.883 --> 00:05:46.010
That means you'll spend less
time on the production side,

00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:47.190
which is probably also gonna mean

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:48.730
that you're gonna spend a lot less money.

00:05:48.730 --> 00:05:52.510
So as much time as you can
spend pondering strategy,

00:05:52.510 --> 00:05:54.850
and consulting with your
colleagues across Yale,

00:05:54.850 --> 00:05:56.603
there's a lot of great folks that,

00:05:57.830 --> 00:06:00.340
that can actually help
you out in this process,

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:02.190
and I'll get to that in a little bit.

00:06:03.327 --> 00:06:04.300
So what did we think?

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:06.370
So I'll share a little bit about how Yale,

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:09.040
how Yale SOM thought about
this, to hopefully help you

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:10.840
on your own podcasting journey.

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:15.210
So we wanted it to be a
community-building exercise,

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:18.090
both in how we put the podcast together,

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:19.600
but also how it lived out in the world.

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:22.130
Meaning, as we produced
the podcast, we wanted

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:25.220
to be connecting students, and
alums, and faculty members,

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:27.800
using various parts of the university.

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:29.420
And so, we really thought it would be

00:06:29.420 --> 00:06:31.180
a community-building exercise.

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:33.650
We wanted it to sustain
and deepen the bonds

00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:37.310
among our students and alums,
and our fans of the school.

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:39.390
I'll say fans, because
they're maybe not necessarily

00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:41.740
within our community,
but people that love SOM.

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:44.990
And then, we also wanted
it to support our mission

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:47.440
for business and society,
that's always the lens

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:49.790
for which we create
content, and share content.

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:53.250
And, of course, finally,
if we're doing all

00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:57.110
that really well, we're probably
attracting the right folks

00:06:57.110 --> 00:06:58.130
to the school, right?

00:06:58.130 --> 00:07:00.230
So prospective students,
getting the right people

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:01.323
to our programs.

00:07:11.476 --> 00:07:14.393
Sorry, I'm gonna just a, all right.

00:07:16.162 --> 00:07:17.890
So I talked a little bit about,

00:07:17.890 --> 00:07:19.730
that was a little bit
about audience, right,

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:22.250
we have this kind of
internal, a lot of internal

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:23.750
and it kind of builds out to the bullseye

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:25.740
of kind of our fans and
prospective students.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:27.860
And then, we wanted to also understand

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:30.140
what the competitive
landscape looked like.

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:31.620
There's a lot of business schools

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:35.090
that are doing similar
things, producing similar

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:38.820
audio formats.

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:40.580
I'd say one of the most successful

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:42.793
is MIT's Data Made to Matter.

00:07:43.691 --> 00:07:46.460
If you're looking to launch
a podcast, I feel like

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:48.610
you should look very closely
to what you're thinking about,

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:50.300
but also look more broadly.

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:53.020
I think logistically we're
probably not gonna be competing

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:57.310
with huge, you know,
five producer podcasts,

00:07:57.310 --> 00:08:00.140
but probably more like the HBS,

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:02.493
Michigan Ross, or MIT models.

00:08:04.053 --> 00:08:06.530
And then, of course, what's
your, what's your USP,

00:08:06.530 --> 00:08:08.630
what's your hypothesis for launching this,

00:08:08.630 --> 00:08:10.970
and kind of blending that all together?

00:08:10.970 --> 00:08:13.010
And so, I think that's
kind of the final step

00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:16.380
in the strategy, after you've
done these sort of goal

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:20.040
exercises, as well as,
checking out your audience

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:22.430
and competitive analysis,
kind of distilling

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:24.890
that into your hypothesis.

00:08:24.890 --> 00:08:26.450
And so, for Career Conversations,

00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:27.893
that's when we came up with,

00:08:28.781 --> 00:08:31.460
you know, we really wanted this to be

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:35.110
something that would reflect
our entire community.

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:38.700
And so, we put students
together with alums

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:42.640
currently at the school,
talking about different,

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:44.963
with their experiences within each sector.

00:08:51.820 --> 00:08:53.790
So pre-production, so the first step in

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:57.160
a podcast kind of launch
or, is your strategy, right?

00:08:57.160 --> 00:08:59.160
That's kind of sitting in a
room, whether it's an hour,

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:01.320
you can take an hour, you
can bring some colleagues in,

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:02.923
spend a few hours, a few months,

00:09:04.490 --> 00:09:05.323
or much longer.

00:09:05.323 --> 00:09:07.210
And then once you're done
with that strategy process,

00:09:07.210 --> 00:09:09.460
what does pre-production look like?

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:10.980
I have to say that I was not prepared

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:13.203
for the amount of time
that this would take.

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:17.390
In fact, so prepare yourself, right?

00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:20.300
It's gonna take you much
longer than you think.

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:22.660
These are just a few of the
things you want to think about

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:24.620
in what I call pre-production.

00:09:24.620 --> 00:09:26.485
Did I mention I'm not an expert?

00:09:26.485 --> 00:09:27.550
(people laughing)

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:29.440
I learned a lot in this process.

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:31.270
You want to think about
your production schedule,

00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:34.150
and really kind of being
as, planning that out

00:09:34.150 --> 00:09:35.150
as much as possible.

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:38.410
This is kind of a glimpse at what

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:41.210
the production schedule looked
like for our season one.

00:09:41.210 --> 00:09:43.427
And you'll see there's various fields for,

00:09:43.427 --> 00:09:45.720
and this is pretty specific,
but I think it's good

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:47.280
to be specific, right?

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:49.620
So you have when we're
releasing something,

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:52.313
who the folks are that
are being interviewed.

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:55.940
In our case, you know,
we had to book a studio,

00:09:55.940 --> 00:09:57.620
we had to schedule Zoom calls, we had,

00:09:57.620 --> 00:10:00.933
sometimes we had a remote
producer, et cetera, et cetera.

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:05.700
So this sort of represents
kind of the bird's eye view.

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:08.233
And then there's a lot of in-person prep,

00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:11.140
and you're developing
scripts and briefing docs.

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:13.310
So let me talk a little bit about that.

00:10:13.310 --> 00:10:15.870
Our podcast, we paired we
a student with an alum.

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:17.290
So the student's usually on campus,

00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:20.980
the alum can be anywhere,
from we've recorded interviews

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:25.370
with folks in San Francisco,
and London, and New York City.

00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:29.000
And so, that takes a little bit of prep.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:31.790
You know, finding a shared time to find

00:10:31.790 --> 00:10:33.810
a time to record would take a little time.

00:10:33.810 --> 00:10:35.930
But we like to do as much
in-person prep as possible.

00:10:35.930 --> 00:10:39.360
And again, this is sort of the
spend more time at the outset

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:40.720
so that when you're in production

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:42.030
you're saving yourself some time.

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:43.470
So in-person prep is really important

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:44.720
for all these interviews.

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:47.730
Developing scripts and briefing docs.

00:10:49.450 --> 00:10:52.070
This is also something I
didn't really anticipate,

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:55.760
but which had really helped
the process, and I feel like

00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:57.530
by mid-season I was really good at it.

00:10:57.530 --> 00:11:00.090
I was really great at
creating a briefing doc.

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:01.960
Oh, I guess you're not
seeing what I'm seeing.

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:03.020
Here we go.

00:11:03.020 --> 00:11:05.890
So creating briefing documents
so that both parties,

00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:08.450
wherever they were in the process,

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:09.810
would understand what was going on.

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:13.420
You'd be shocked to know
how many folks on the day

00:11:13.420 --> 00:11:16.210
of the podcast recording
thought is this video,

00:11:16.210 --> 00:11:17.670
or is this just, you know?

00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:21.400
It was still, you could
never be too specific

00:11:21.400 --> 00:11:23.140
to what you think is obvious.

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:25.390
So I created a briefing doc,
and I would encourage you,

00:11:25.390 --> 00:11:27.860
and wherever you are in your podcast prep,

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:28.970
to create briefing docs.

00:11:28.970 --> 00:11:33.098
Be as specific as possible,
and also, scripts.

00:11:33.098 --> 00:11:35.060
I think it's sort of
obvious stating the obvious,

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:36.330
but, I think scripts are really important,

00:11:36.330 --> 00:11:37.860
reviewing those scripts
as much as possible

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:38.960
with your interviewer.

00:11:41.010 --> 00:11:43.690
Securing audio, tape sync, studio time.

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:45.260
I think we'll talk a
little bit about kind of

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:48.240
your quality requirements, but I think

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:49.540
that's also really important.

00:11:49.540 --> 00:11:51.463
And then, also,

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:54.440
Outlook reminders, I think we're all fans,

00:11:54.440 --> 00:11:56.540
or not fans of Outlook, but we all use it.

00:11:57.820 --> 00:11:59.730
I ended up using Outlook quite a bit

00:11:59.730 --> 00:12:01.870
to sort of be that locus
for all that information

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:03.277
to organize for everybody
that was involved,

00:12:03.277 --> 00:12:04.677
and that was really helpful.

00:12:11.740 --> 00:12:13.690
Here's one of our students, Max Dworin,

00:12:14.870 --> 00:12:16.550
and he was interviewing somebody

00:12:16.550 --> 00:12:17.730
who was obviously not there.

00:12:17.730 --> 00:12:21.080
And so, most of our
episodes featured a student.

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:23.910
In our studio at SOM, we're
lucky to have a studio space

00:12:24.770 --> 00:12:27.540
and alum somewhere else.

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:29.700
We thought most episodes
will look like this,

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:33.330
happy students, seamless,
press a button, good-to-go.

00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:34.970
But, that was not really
not always the case.

00:12:34.970 --> 00:12:37.290
Sometimes, I actually had to,

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:38.590
you know, the studio wasn't available,

00:12:38.590 --> 00:12:40.780
the tape sync person canceled,
and I had to drag somebody

00:12:40.780 --> 00:12:44.480
in my office, run to the Apple
Store to grab a microphone,

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:47.733
quickly setup a Zencastr feed,
and just record at my desk.

00:12:48.870 --> 00:12:51.030
You know, you never
know what's gonna happen

00:12:51.030 --> 00:12:51.863
with these things.

00:12:51.863 --> 00:12:55.323
So for production, I would
say, you know, consider,

00:12:56.270 --> 00:12:58.150
ask yourself a few questions.

00:12:58.150 --> 00:13:00.020
What's your audio style?

00:13:00.020 --> 00:13:02.800
What's your tolerance for

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:05.870
great audio quality,
or so-so audio quality?

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:08.100
Which probably depends on your budget.

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:09.990
And then have you consulted an expert?

00:13:09.990 --> 00:13:11.390
I think there's some
folks here that work at

00:13:11.390 --> 00:13:13.460
the Yale Broadcasting Center.

00:13:13.460 --> 00:13:14.453
Guy's over there.

00:13:15.430 --> 00:13:17.310
Of course, we have one at SOM,

00:13:17.310 --> 00:13:20.530
and I hear CTL also has one.

00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:22.200
Those are all great resources.

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:25.480
I would encourage you
to seek out any resource

00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:27.240
that you can, as a consultant.

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:29.623
But, I think also at the end of the day,

00:13:30.860 --> 00:13:33.610
consider that spectrum
of quality, and maybe DIY

00:13:33.610 --> 00:13:35.123
is the way to go, right?

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:43.310
When it comes to post-production,

00:13:43.310 --> 00:13:45.333
your post-production checklist,

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:49.130
there's a lot of things to consider.

00:13:49.130 --> 00:13:51.550
Are you gonna be editing
and mixing this yourself,

00:13:51.550 --> 00:13:52.940
or are you gonna hire somebody?

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:54.298
Hire somebody.

00:13:54.298 --> 00:13:55.180
(people laughing)

00:13:55.180 --> 00:13:58.510
What's an MP3 file,
and what's ID3 tagging?

00:13:58.510 --> 00:14:01.690
You should probably figure
out what those things are.

00:14:01.690 --> 00:14:03.310
Everything is available by Google.

00:14:03.310 --> 00:14:04.763
But again, get an editor.

00:14:05.710 --> 00:14:08.160
Show notes, I think this
is another important part

00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:11.330
of the process when it comes
to how you're promoting

00:14:11.330 --> 00:14:13.480
and how you're offering,
and kind of augmenting

00:14:13.480 --> 00:14:15.600
that content that you're sharing.

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:17.300
Transcripts are really
important, you know,

00:14:17.300 --> 00:14:18.930
accessibility at Yale is really important,

00:14:18.930 --> 00:14:20.670
it's something we take into
consideration all the time,

00:14:20.670 --> 00:14:25.250
so absolutely, positively,
transcribe your episodes.

00:14:25.250 --> 00:14:29.470
When it comes to reviewing
and approving episodes,

00:14:29.470 --> 00:14:31.490
obviously, we want things to
be as seamless as possible,

00:14:31.490 --> 00:14:32.323
and pretty quick.

00:14:32.323 --> 00:14:34.200
But, I always like to
give a courtesy review

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:36.330
to the folks that I've interviewed

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:37.763
or were part of the process.

00:14:39.070 --> 00:14:41.450
And then, audiograms and promotion

00:14:41.450 --> 00:14:43.293
we'll touch on a little bit.

00:14:45.770 --> 00:14:48.633
So this is, let's see if I can play this.

00:14:49.470 --> 00:14:51.638
- [Michael] Having meaning in my work

00:14:51.638 --> 00:14:54.630
is really vitally important,
and it's the main motivation

00:14:54.630 --> 00:14:57.590
for becoming and doing
what I do every day.

00:14:57.590 --> 00:15:00.820
Fortunately, in healthcare,
it's been relatively easy

00:15:00.820 --> 00:15:02.750
for me to find meaning in the work.

00:15:02.750 --> 00:15:05.120
Being able to sit with
patients and their families,

00:15:05.120 --> 00:15:08.560
and really see the effect
that we have on their lives

00:15:08.560 --> 00:15:10.310
is a way that provide real meaning.

00:15:10.310 --> 00:15:11.143
But, I think...

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:15.670
- So I just shared this
one example of promotion

00:15:15.670 --> 00:15:19.860
that we found to be successful,
which is an audiogram.

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:22.160
There's free apps to do
these sorts of things.

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:27.130
Headliner app is one of
the ones that we've used,

00:15:27.130 --> 00:15:29.323
so that's something that we've utilized.

00:15:36.310 --> 00:15:38.053
So publishing and promoting.

00:15:39.570 --> 00:15:42.080
Obviously, there's so many
ways and so many directions

00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:43.450
you can go with this.

00:15:43.450 --> 00:15:46.700
We tend to write a news story,
and we've templatized it,

00:15:46.700 --> 00:15:48.730
so I don't have to reinvent
the wheel every time

00:15:48.730 --> 00:15:50.120
we launch an episode.

00:15:50.120 --> 00:15:53.640
We've decided to launch a
new episode every two weeks,

00:15:53.640 --> 00:15:56.160
so rather than just dump them all at once.

00:15:56.160 --> 00:15:57.610
For us, we thought it
would be great to kind

00:15:57.610 --> 00:16:00.640
of slowly acquire
followers and subscribers,

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:02.970
so that we can increase our listenership

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:06.370
and create more opportunities
to promote along the way.

00:16:06.370 --> 00:16:08.230
However, if you already
have a great following,

00:16:08.230 --> 00:16:11.041
maybe just dumping a whole
season at once is the way to go,

00:16:11.041 --> 00:16:12.743
and then promoting it thereafter.

00:16:13.910 --> 00:16:17.810
We create news articles for
each one, I embed a SoundCloud

00:16:17.810 --> 00:16:22.040
listening tool within that,
and we often will link out

00:16:22.040 --> 00:16:25.150
to some other resources and show notes.

00:16:25.150 --> 00:16:28.360
You'll see we post, we often
post on Instagram and Twitter.

00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:30.540
And then, in the case of Dr. Apkon,

00:16:30.540 --> 00:16:33.900
who was one of our, one
of our interviewees,

00:16:33.900 --> 00:16:35.610
he works at the Tufts Medical Center,

00:16:35.610 --> 00:16:38.067
so they were kind enough to promote that.

00:16:38.067 --> 00:16:40.780
And so, sort of coordinating
with your subjects,

00:16:40.780 --> 00:16:43.003
obviously, is sort of a great,

00:16:44.300 --> 00:16:45.663
a great thing to always do.

00:16:46.740 --> 00:16:49.193
So lessons learned.

00:16:50.190 --> 00:16:52.790
Set aside twice the
time you think you need

00:16:52.790 --> 00:16:54.400
to do anything, the whole thing.

00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:55.660
Strategy, twice the time.

00:16:55.660 --> 00:16:57.690
Production, pre-production, launching it,

00:16:57.690 --> 00:17:00.173
it's gonna take three times the time.

00:17:00.173 --> 00:17:01.603
3x your budget, if you can.

00:17:02.560 --> 00:17:03.773
Create a trailer.

00:17:05.180 --> 00:17:07.130
What I learned sort of halfway through

00:17:07.130 --> 00:17:10.840
the process of launching was
that getting your feed setup

00:17:10.840 --> 00:17:13.870
with Apple, for example,
takes quite awhile.

00:17:13.870 --> 00:17:15.970
It can take anywhere from
three days to three weeks,

00:17:15.970 --> 00:17:17.710
it's sort of a black box.

00:17:17.710 --> 00:17:21.020
The trailer sort of is your test,

00:17:21.020 --> 00:17:22.240
your test episode, if you will.

00:17:22.240 --> 00:17:25.600
So if you're gonna launch
anything through the Apple Store,

00:17:25.600 --> 00:17:28.770
I recommend you do a launch
with a trailer first.

00:17:28.770 --> 00:17:30.880
And it can be a 30 second, or a 10 second,

00:17:30.880 --> 00:17:34.000
a little bit of audio promoting your

00:17:35.050 --> 00:17:35.883
podcast.

00:17:35.883 --> 00:17:38.190
But then, usually, thereafter,
it's a pretty quick,

00:17:38.190 --> 00:17:40.133
a couple hours at most.

00:17:41.330 --> 00:17:44.600
And then always have a Plan B,

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:47.820
or a Plan C, and a Plan D.

00:17:47.820 --> 00:17:49.550
You know, I can't...

00:17:49.550 --> 00:17:53.665
As my colleagues Emily and
Jonathan know, who are here,

00:17:53.665 --> 00:17:56.527
who have heard me kind of go
into your offices and say,

00:17:56.527 --> 00:17:59.447
"Can you believe Dr. Apkon's
canceled for the third time,

00:17:59.447 --> 00:18:00.347
"can you believe?"

00:18:01.994 --> 00:18:03.990
There are so many variables, and

00:18:04.880 --> 00:18:06.400
if you can simplify your production,

00:18:06.400 --> 00:18:07.780
the better off you'll be.

00:18:07.780 --> 00:18:10.450
I think going into it I
thought we don't need a host,

00:18:10.450 --> 00:18:12.910
this is great, I can pair
a student with an alum,

00:18:12.910 --> 00:18:14.240
this is perfect!

00:18:14.240 --> 00:18:17.047
However, what I didn't
anticipate was not having a host

00:18:17.047 --> 00:18:18.647
and I know you're a host, right,

00:18:20.225 --> 00:18:21.880
host's are great, they're important,

00:18:21.880 --> 00:18:24.430
I sort of wish I had thought
that, maybe, we do need a host.

00:18:24.430 --> 00:18:26.400
So not having a host, here's what happens.

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:28.980
I have to prep in-person every student.

00:18:28.980 --> 00:18:30.540
When you're in the studio with them,

00:18:30.540 --> 00:18:33.440
you need to interrupt them,
remind how to say something,

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:35.000
how to do something.

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:36.520
Some of our students don't have,

00:18:36.520 --> 00:18:39.270
you know, English maybe
isn't their first language.

00:18:39.270 --> 00:18:41.860
And so, you have to do
many, many, many takes.

00:18:41.860 --> 00:18:43.720
Which is fine, and I think
that's important for us,

00:18:43.720 --> 00:18:45.770
we want that breathe of
international students

00:18:45.770 --> 00:18:47.150
are important to us.

00:18:47.150 --> 00:18:48.960
It just takes a little
bit more prep, right,

00:18:48.960 --> 00:18:50.270
when you don't have a host.

00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:52.490
So a lesson learned

00:18:52.490 --> 00:18:54.920
Also, when you're scheduling interviews

00:18:54.920 --> 00:18:56.900
with faculty members who are very busy,

00:18:56.900 --> 00:18:59.780
with alums who are eminent in their field,

00:18:59.780 --> 00:19:01.170
these are busy folks.

00:19:01.170 --> 00:19:02.940
They certainly want to help
out and engage with you.

00:19:02.940 --> 00:19:06.160
However, they're probably
going to cancel on you

00:19:06.160 --> 00:19:07.260
at the last moment.

00:19:07.260 --> 00:19:09.420
Sometimes, with very little notice.

00:19:09.420 --> 00:19:10.900
And so, what does that mean?

00:19:10.900 --> 00:19:12.410
It doesn't seem like a
big deal, you have a free,

00:19:12.410 --> 00:19:14.260
now you have a free hour in your schedule.

00:19:14.260 --> 00:19:17.120
However, when you're
a producer, that means

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:19.260
telling the studio, re-booking the studio,

00:19:19.260 --> 00:19:21.240
telling the tape sync
person, this happened to be

00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:23.590
with a person in London, I had to cancel

00:19:23.590 --> 00:19:25.420
the tape sync person in London.

00:19:25.420 --> 00:19:27.680
I had to cancel our on-site.

00:19:27.680 --> 00:19:28.970
I also had to tell our student.

00:19:28.970 --> 00:19:31.120
And so, there's this whole series

00:19:31.120 --> 00:19:33.170
of dominoes that falls,
and you have to do it,

00:19:33.170 --> 00:19:34.930
in the case of Dr. Apkon, three times.

00:19:34.930 --> 00:19:37.230
He happens to be an
amazing, very nice person,

00:19:37.230 --> 00:19:38.280
so that helped.

00:19:38.280 --> 00:19:41.593
However, it made it, it
makes it extremely stressful.

00:19:45.500 --> 00:19:47.520
I will also kind of, you know,

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:49.700
I mentioned budget, triple your budget.

00:19:49.700 --> 00:19:52.030
Now, you can bootstrap
this, and I encourage you

00:19:52.030 --> 00:19:53.840
to bootstrap it, right?

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:57.053
Not everything has to be kind
of a very high-end production.

00:19:58.190 --> 00:20:01.300
You know, get Zencastr.

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:03.130
Get a microphone from the Apple Store.

00:20:03.130 --> 00:20:06.500
If you walked out of here
today, you could probably launch

00:20:06.500 --> 00:20:08.070
your first episode in three hours,

00:20:08.070 --> 00:20:10.090
if you just sort of, kind of, again,

00:20:10.090 --> 00:20:12.400
spend a little bit of
time thinking about it.

00:20:12.400 --> 00:20:15.890
There's enough tools and
platforms out there to do that.

00:20:15.890 --> 00:20:18.640
And I would actually
encourage you to do that first

00:20:18.640 --> 00:20:21.420
as an exercise, I think
that's really important.

00:20:21.420 --> 00:20:23.640
I think so many of us think
we want to launch a podcast,

00:20:23.640 --> 00:20:26.470
and don't really kind of think
through the amount of time

00:20:26.470 --> 00:20:28.932
that it will take out of
your kind of workflow.

00:20:28.932 --> 00:20:30.832
And it will take a great deal of time.

00:20:36.170 --> 00:20:38.360
- [Female in Audience] What is
your budget, Amy, for these?

00:20:38.360 --> 00:20:40.260
- That's a great question,
and actually I had,

00:20:40.260 --> 00:20:41.660
I wasn't able to

00:20:42.560 --> 00:20:43.420
add my

00:20:44.450 --> 00:20:46.440
slide, I can probably
pull it up in a quick sec,

00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:48.240
so I will actually share this with you.

00:20:48.240 --> 00:20:51.910
I created a pseudo budget I
wanted to share with you all.

00:20:51.910 --> 00:20:55.380
Think about your launch costs,
think about your annual costs

00:20:55.380 --> 00:20:58.430
and think about your recurring costs.

00:20:58.430 --> 00:21:00.890
So I would say it netted
out, it can net out

00:21:00.890 --> 00:21:03.770
to about $1500 an episode.

00:21:03.770 --> 00:21:07.380
And the reason being is,
if you hire maybe somebody

00:21:07.380 --> 00:21:09.430
to get you started, and
if you have a budget,

00:21:09.430 --> 00:21:11.170
initial budget, I think
it's really important

00:21:11.170 --> 00:21:13.280
to spend a little bit on
hiring an external producer,

00:21:13.280 --> 00:21:15.700
which we did, to help get us kick-started.

00:21:15.700 --> 00:21:16.740
It gave us confidence.

00:21:16.740 --> 00:21:18.960
So I think that is really helpful.

00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:20.950
You're gonna want to
think about music rights,

00:21:20.950 --> 00:21:25.090
or composing some music, or
you know, something like that.

00:21:25.090 --> 00:21:27.250
You're gonna want to think about...

00:21:28.410 --> 00:21:30.190
Sorry, my slides aren't
advancing as easily

00:21:30.190 --> 00:21:32.670
as I thought they would, there we go.

00:21:32.670 --> 00:21:34.490
So you're gonna want
to think about design.

00:21:34.490 --> 00:21:36.690
You know, I didn't come
up with that design.

00:21:36.690 --> 00:21:38.660
That was from a very kind of,

00:21:38.660 --> 00:21:41.240
another really fantastic graphic designer

00:21:41.240 --> 00:21:43.130
who created that look and feel for us.

00:21:43.130 --> 00:21:44.560
But we wanted this to be a flagship,

00:21:44.560 --> 00:21:46.563
so we wanted to invest into it, right?

00:21:47.451 --> 00:21:48.850
You're gonna think about music,

00:21:48.850 --> 00:21:51.050
you're gonna think about design,

00:21:51.050 --> 00:21:53.610
you're gonna think
about hosting platforms.

00:21:53.610 --> 00:21:55.530
You're gonna think about
maybe you want to do

00:21:55.530 --> 00:21:57.893
some paid marketing,
paid social marketing.

00:21:58.750 --> 00:22:00.730
I mean, time is an important resource.

00:22:00.730 --> 00:22:02.110
If you were to monetize
that, that would be

00:22:02.110 --> 00:22:03.610
a great deal of time, as well.

00:22:04.630 --> 00:22:07.060
So there's kind of a launch budget.

00:22:07.060 --> 00:22:08.600
There's a per episode budget, and then

00:22:08.600 --> 00:22:09.610
there's some annual fees.

00:22:09.610 --> 00:22:13.250
So if you decide to go with,
say, Libsyn or Blueberry.

00:22:13.250 --> 00:22:16.620
I know Yale Broadcasting
Center uses Blueberry.

00:22:16.620 --> 00:22:18.600
We have been using Libsyn.

00:22:18.600 --> 00:22:20.040
These are platforms that if you want

00:22:20.040 --> 00:22:23.490
to disseminate your
episodes to many platforms,

00:22:23.490 --> 00:22:24.850
it makes it very easy.

00:22:24.850 --> 00:22:26.930
However, there are recurring costs,

00:22:26.930 --> 00:22:29.620
anywhere from $20 to $30 a month,

00:22:29.620 --> 00:22:33.133
to enterprise level can be about $3500.

00:22:34.830 --> 00:22:37.190
I don't think it's unreasonable to think

00:22:37.190 --> 00:22:39.790
that perhaps Yale at some
point might have sort of

00:22:39.790 --> 00:22:42.160
an enterprise offering
that we could all tap into.

00:22:42.160 --> 00:22:44.810
And I'm hoping that being
here today and talking

00:22:44.810 --> 00:22:47.270
with all of you from my
non-expert point of view,

00:22:47.270 --> 00:22:50.160
and with all of you, maybe
we can kind of think about

00:22:50.160 --> 00:22:52.320
who here is doing what we're doing,

00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:53.810
who is planning to do what we're doing,

00:22:53.810 --> 00:22:56.010
and maybe there's some cost
savings in pulling together

00:22:56.010 --> 00:22:59.010
and appealing to Yale to
something kind in a shared way.

00:22:59.010 --> 00:23:01.670
And coordinating with
Yale Broadcast, and CTL,

00:23:01.670 --> 00:23:03.770
and all the other units across the school.

00:23:08.880 --> 00:23:11.310
So these are just a couple of platforms

00:23:11.310 --> 00:23:13.150
that have made my life really easy.

00:23:13.150 --> 00:23:15.310
This is not an exhaustive
list, this is, you know,

00:23:15.310 --> 00:23:17.010
this is pretty easily Google-able.

00:23:17.890 --> 00:23:21.120
I wanted to give you a really
short kind of crib sheet for,

00:23:21.120 --> 00:23:23.150
again, if you wanted to leave here today,

00:23:23.150 --> 00:23:25.800
to spend the rest of the
afternoon in your office,

00:23:25.800 --> 00:23:27.900
and just crank it out, you could do it

00:23:27.900 --> 00:23:29.363
with these things, right?

00:23:30.416 --> 00:23:34.580
Podcaster, that Apple.com
has pretty much any resource

00:23:34.580 --> 00:23:36.030
and every resource you could imagine,

00:23:36.030 --> 00:23:39.980
to know how to launch a podcast for Apple.

00:23:39.980 --> 00:23:42.540
It will give you the
aspect ratio for the images

00:23:42.540 --> 00:23:46.360
that you need for the square
of your podcast, a image.

00:23:46.360 --> 00:23:48.920
It will give for the banner ad, as well.

00:23:48.920 --> 00:23:51.410
Libsyn is a great
podcasting hosting platform

00:23:51.410 --> 00:23:52.990
that I mentioned.

00:23:52.990 --> 00:23:57.140
Headliner.app will handle
audiograms for you.

00:23:57.140 --> 00:23:58.260
Audacity

00:23:59.340 --> 00:24:02.060
will edit, if you decide
you want to edit yourself.

00:24:02.060 --> 00:24:04.089
I'm not that brave.

